WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:01.590
-: The last variation we'll take a look

00:01.590 --> 00:04.110
at is comparing two commits.

00:04.110 --> 00:06.660
If you want to know what changed between two commits

00:06.660 --> 00:08.760
not necessarily HEAD, right?

00:08.760 --> 00:10.980
It could be any commit in the past.

00:10.980 --> 00:13.500
We can use the same syntax, git diff

00:13.500 --> 00:18.150
and then some commit hash.., some other commit hash.

00:18.150 --> 00:20.973
So why don't we go back to master.

00:22.770 --> 00:23.603
Git log.

00:23.603 --> 00:25.260
Let's do oneline.

00:25.260 --> 00:28.680
If I want to know what changed between, I don't know,

00:28.680 --> 00:33.680
add red, so that commit, and add blue and purple, sure.

00:35.190 --> 00:37.020
If I want to know what changed there,

00:37.020 --> 00:39.300
I need to start by copying one of these commit hashes.

00:39.300 --> 00:41.910
It's kind of annoying actually to keep track

00:41.910 --> 00:42.960
of both of them.

00:42.960 --> 00:44.670
So I'm just going to paste it in this file.

00:44.670 --> 00:47.970
And then the second one I wanted was this commit hash.

00:47.970 --> 00:49.890
All right, let's see what happened there.

00:49.890 --> 00:54.470
So git diff and then the first one, .., the second one.

00:57.901 --> 01:01.080
And we see the only file that changed was colors.

01:01.080 --> 01:02.280
That's the only file really.

01:02.280 --> 01:04.200
Well we did introduce the numbers file

01:04.200 --> 01:06.900
but after this commit, so it

01:06.900 --> 01:09.630
didn't even exist in either of these commits.

01:09.630 --> 01:11.400
But here you can see the changes, right?

01:11.400 --> 01:13.200
We added all this stuff

01:13.200 --> 01:15.906
into the file between those two commits

01:15.906 --> 01:18.750
and they're not necessarily consecutive commits

01:18.750 --> 01:21.480
it's just all changes between those two commits.

01:21.480 --> 01:23.280
And this also works if I have a space

01:23.280 --> 01:26.580
just like with two branches, same diff.

01:26.580 --> 01:29.310
Alright, so to recap those different versions

01:29.310 --> 01:31.410
we can compare changes between two commits.

01:31.410 --> 01:34.560
The order matters just like it does with two branches.

01:34.560 --> 01:36.830
So all changes between all files

01:36.830 --> 01:41.310
for two different branches or two different commits.

01:41.310 --> 01:43.140
Then we saw that we can do the same thing

01:43.140 --> 01:46.440
but actually narrow it down to one file or two or three.

01:46.440 --> 01:48.810
We just call those files out afterwards.

01:48.810 --> 01:51.360
So that also works for branches and commits.

01:51.360 --> 01:53.760
And we saw that we can use the -- staged

01:53.760 --> 01:58.760
or cached option to only see the changes that are staged.

01:58.770 --> 02:02.340
To see unstaged changes only, we can use git diff

02:02.340 --> 02:05.040
and then to see all changes since the last commit,

02:05.040 --> 02:07.050
so everything that's new in the working directory,

02:07.050 --> 02:09.933
staged or unstaged, git diff HEAD.
